window.LEARN_CONTENT=window.LEARN_CONTENT||{};window.LEARN_CONTENT["a-e62c8f8bdac9e5df"]={"zh":"<p><a id=\"sites-administration\"></a></p> <h1 id=\"sites\">Sites 管理</h1> <blockquote> <p>完整文档索引请参阅 <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt（需联网）</a>。如需查看文档的 Markdown 版本，可在页面 URL 末尾追加 <code>.md</code>。</p> </blockquote> <p>有关当前工作区设置、权限和功能可用性，请参阅帮助中心的<a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001338-managing-chatgpt-sites-for-your-workspace\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">在工作区中管理 ChatGPT Sites（需联网）</a>。</p> <p><span id=\"what-costs-and-limits-should-we-plan-for\"></span></p> <p>使用本指南确定工作区中谁可以创建、修改和使用 Sites，以及组织应如何审核和维护这些站点。有关构建或使用站点的信息，请参阅 <a href=\"#/article/a-3c6726ad641677d6\">Sites</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"before-you-begin\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_1\">开始之前</h2> <p>登录你负责管理的工作区。确定谁可以创建和发布站点、哪些受众适合访问，以及由谁负责审核、支持和处理移除请求。</p> <p>从一个具体用例开始。例如，展示已批准参考资料的入职培训站点，与收集员工信息的请求跟踪器，或使用已连接应用的仪表板，所需审核并不相同。请先就以下事项达成一致：</p> <ul> <li>目标受众，以及是否适合开放外部或公众访问。</li> <li>站点会展示、收集、存储或发送到其他服务的信息。</li> <li>负责站点内容、应用行为和持续支持的人员。</li> <li>首次发布前以及发生重要变更后需要完成的审核。</li> </ul> <p><a id=\"how-sites-work\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"sites_1\">Sites 的工作方式</h2> <p>站点可以包含页面、应用代码和访客信息。分享之前，请审核站点会发布和收集哪些内容。有关构建和发布流程，请参阅<a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001339\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">创建和管理 ChatGPT Sites（需联网）</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"what-you-can-build\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_2\">可以构建什么</h3> <p>Sites 可用于构建参考页面、表单、跟踪器和交互式仪表板。审核内容应依据应用实际执行的操作。参考页面需要审核内容和受众；表单还需要决定要收集哪些提交内容以及谁可以查看；连接应用的仪表板则需要审核相关账户、权限和信息。</p> <p><a id=\"information-stored-or-exposed-by-a-site\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_3\">站点存储或暴露的信息</h3> <p>提示、对话上下文以及上传或引用的文件中的信息，都可能成为站点的一部分。请检查生成的页面、示例记录、下载内容和生成内容，确认其中没有不应向目标受众展示的信息。</p> <p>要区分复制到站点中的信息与通过连接检索的信息。例如，上传的电子表格可能成为共享快照；访客使用自己的已连接账户时，访问权限可能与构建者不同。请测试实际行为；构建者自己的预览结果不能证明其他访客能看到什么。</p> <p>有关构建工作流程的信息，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-e0f092cc982945c2\">数据与连接</a>。</p> <p>有关托管和数据处理提供商的信息，请参阅 OpenAI 的<a href=\"https://openai.com/policies/sub-processor-list/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">子处理方列表（需联网）</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"enable-sites-and-permissions\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"sites_2\">启用 Sites 和设置权限</h2> <p><span id=\"enable-sites-and-choose-sharing-permissions\"></span></p> <p>请检查三个层级的控制措施：工作区权限、站点的分享和编辑权限，以及应用本身实现的权限。</p> <figure> <p class=\"media-note\">三个控制层级：工作区权限控制创建和发布；站点权限控制查看和编辑；应用代码控制对记录和操作的访问。 · <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://developers.openai.com/images/codex/sites/controls.webp\">联网查看原始媒体</a></p> <figcaption>三个控制层级：工作区权限控制创建和发布；站点权限控制查看和编辑；应用代码控制对记录和操作的访问。 · <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/images/codex/sites/controls.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">打开官网原始媒体</a></figcaption> </figure> <p><a id=\"workspace-admin-controls\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_4\">工作区管理员控制</h3> <p>工作区权限决定谁可以创建和发布站点。应分别审核公开发布和外部邀请：允许某个指定的外部访客访问，与让互联网上任何人都能访问站点，是两种不同的决定。</p> <p>有关配置步骤和当前角色权限，请遵循<a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001338-managing-chatgpt-sites-for-your-workspace\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">工作区管理指南（需联网）</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"site-owner-and-collaborator-controls\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_5\">站点所有者和协作者控制</h3> <p>使用站点的分享控制，决定谁可以查看站点，以及谁可以帮助维护站点。</p> <p><a id=\"audience-and-sharing\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_6\">受众和分享</h4> <p>站点的受众设置决定谁可以打开站点。应选择与站点提供的信息和功能相符的受众。缩小访问范围时，请检查所有仍然有效的授权。即使移除了某人的单独邀请，对方仍可能通过群组、整个工作区的分享设置或公开发布获得访问权限。</p> <p>更改访问权限后，请使用目标访客的实际身份检查站点。有关分享流程，请参阅<a href=\"#/article/a-fc9cd7880ec6ce59\">发布和分享</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"editors-and-collaboration\"></a></p> <h4 id=\"_7\">编辑者和协作</h4> <p>所有者可以邀请符合条件的工作区成员成为编辑者。首次发布由所有者完成；此后编辑者可以发布更新。访问权限和仅限所有者使用的设置仍由所有者管理。</p> <p>请约定编辑者在发布前如何申请和记录审核。编辑者之后可以发布新版本，而无需额外的所有者批准步骤，因此组织的审核流程必须考虑哪些人拥有编辑权限。</p> <p><a id=\"application-permissions\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_8\">应用权限</h3> <p>站点访问权限与对单条记录的访问权限是两回事。若应用数据是私有的，应在服务器端代码中执行记录级访问控制，并使用普通访客账户进行测试。</p> <p>例如，请求跟踪器可以允许所有工作区成员打开站点，同时只让每个人查看自己的请求。应用在返回记录或接受操作时必须检查这条规则。隐藏页面元素并不能真正实施访问控制。请使用两个权限不同的账户测试，确认它们无法访问彼此不应查看的记录。</p> <p><a id=\"configure-connections\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_9\">配置连接</h2> <p><span id=\"enable-tenant-access-to-connected-apps\"></span></p> <p><span id=\"check-tenant-access-if-plugins-are-blocked\"></span></p> <p><span id=\"allow-an-additional-destination-for-one-site\"></span> <span id=\"credentials-and-connected-service-access\"></span></p> <p>创建或分享站点本身不会授权其访问已连接应用。请根据帮助中心的<a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001338-managing-chatgpt-sites-for-your-workspace\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">已连接应用指南（需联网）</a>，审核组织审批、插件设置，以及每位访客已连接的账户和权限。</p> <p>审核站点时也要检查其中使用的连接。确认它会使用哪些服务、请求哪些信息，以及由谁批准这些访问。站点的受众设置与已连接服务的权限应分开评估。</p> <p>不要将凭据放进提示、附件或站点可见内容中。如果你对网络访问有疑问，请联系工作区管理员。</p> <p><a id=\"verify-the-visitor-experience\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_10\">验证访客体验</h3> <p>已连接的站点应限制在其所属工作区内。在工作区内扩大分享范围之前，请使用另一位符合条件、并通过自己的账户连接服务的工作区成员进行测试。确认站点展示的信息符合该访客现有的访问权限。</p> <p>还要检查访客未授予所请求的连接权限时会看到什么。说明哪些功能需要该连接，并确保站点不会把缺失的数据伪装成成功结果。关于支持的连接和访问要求，请遵循当前帮助中心指南。</p> <p><a id=\"review-and-manage-sites\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"sites_3\">审核和管理 Sites</h2> <p><span id=\"workspace-visibility-and-audit-records\"></span> <span id=\"publishing-approval-and-network-controls\"></span></p> <p><a id=\"saved-versions-and-publishing\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_11\">已保存版本和发布</h3> <p>编辑并保存一个版本，与发布该版本是两件不同的事。先检查更新后的预览，准备好后再发布给访客。发送编辑指令不会立即更改已发布的站点。</p> <figure> <p class=\"media-note\">推荐流程：编辑并保存、审核并测试、明确发布该版本，最后以访客身份检查线上站点。审核是组织层面的步骤，不是产品自带的审批门槛。 · <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://developers.openai.com/images/codex/sites/publishing.webp\">联网查看原始媒体</a></p> <figcaption>推荐流程：编辑并保存、审核并测试、明确发布该版本，最后以访客身份检查线上站点。审核是组织层面的步骤，不是产品自带的审批门槛。 · <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/images/codex/sites/publishing.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">打开官网原始媒体</a></figcaption> </figure> <p>首次发布和之后的更新都应采用以下流程：</p> <ol> <li>描述要做的更改，并检查已保存的版本。</li> <li>检查受此次更改影响的内容、受众、应用权限和连接。</li> <li>完成组织要求的审核，然后由所有者或符合条件的编辑者发布该版本。首次发布必须由所有者完成。</li> <li>以目标访客的身份打开已发布的站点，确认预期内容和行为均可正常使用。</li> </ol> <p>审核步骤是建议采用的组织流程，不会额外增加产品审批门槛。</p> <p><a id=\"shared-responsibilities\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_12\">共同责任</h3> <p>成功生成或发布站点，并不能证明站点符合组织要求。请明确由谁负责审核内容、访问规则、生成的应用代码和已连接服务。审核者应具备与站点数据及用例相符的专业知识。</p> <p><a id=\"review-before-publishing\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_13\">发布前审核</h3> <p>发布前请审核目标受众、生成的代码、访客提交内容和连接，并测试预期访客与非预期访客的体验。发生重要变更后应重复审核。</p> <p>例如，团队仪表板对构建者显示正常，但可能将上传的客户名单暴露给范围过大的受众；请求表单可能收集了正确的字段，却允许一位访客读取另一位访客提交的信息。请同时审核页面上展示的内容，以及应用如何处理数据和操作。</p> <p>发布前请检查：</p> <ul> <li><strong>内容：</strong> 检查生成的文本、图片、链接、上传文件和示例数据，确认其准确且适合分享。</li> <li><strong>受众：</strong> 确认谁可以打开站点；适用时也要考虑外部访客和公众。</li> <li><strong>记录和操作：</strong> 验证访客只能读取和修改自己有权访问的信息。</li> <li><strong>表单和提交内容：</strong> 决定要收集什么、信息将发送到哪里、谁可以查看，以及访客如何询问相关问题。</li> <li><strong>连接和凭据：</strong> 检查使用的服务、请求的访问权限，以及页面、下载内容或错误消息中可能暴露的信息。</li> <li><strong>访客体验：</strong> 使用不同的访客账户检查预期流程以及被拒绝访问时的情况。</li> </ul> <p><a id=\"ongoing-maintenance\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_14\">持续维护</h3> <p>为每个生产站点指定负责人，并建立报告访问、数据或服务问题的渠道。与工作区管理员共同规划负责人交接和站点退役事宜。</p> <p>当站点新增受众、编辑者、数据源、连接、表单或应用功能时，应重新审核。定期检查受众和编辑者名单是否仍符合站点用途、内容是否仍然有效，以及目标访客是否仍能正常使用连接。</p> <p><a id=\"troubleshoot-access\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_15\">排查访问问题</h3> <p>从受影响人员的账户、角色和尝试执行的操作开始排查。创建者能成功预览，并不能证明其他人也可以查看、编辑、发布站点或使用连接。</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>现象</th> <th>检查内容</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>成员无法创建或发布站点</td> <td>确认当前工作区、功能是否可用，以及该成员角色具有哪些权限。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>访客无法打开站点</td> <td>检查站点受众设置，并确认访客使用的是获得访问权的账户登录。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>编辑者无法发布</td> <td>检查编辑权限、工作区权限，以及所有者是否已完成首次发布。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>创建者能使用连接，其他访客却不能</td> <td>确认站点仅对所属工作区开放，并确认访客属于该工作区。检查访客已连接的账户、服务权限、授权，以及相关组织和插件设置。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>移除邀请后某人仍能查看站点</td> <td>检查是否仍有群组、整个工作区共享或公开访问权限。</td> </tr> <tr> <td>已发布的站点没有显示预期的编辑</td> <td>确认已发布的是预期版本，然后打开线上站点检查结果。</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>如需查看可用的工作区控制项或排查访问问题，请遵循<a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001338-managing-chatgpt-sites-for-your-workspace\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">帮助中心指南（需联网）</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"security-and-privacy\"></a></p> <h2 id=\"_16\">安全与隐私</h2> <p><span id=\"certifications-encryption-and-customer-managed-keys\"></span></p> <p><span id=\"applicable-terms-and-data-processing-agreements\"></span></p> <p>选择在站点中存储哪些信息之前，请根据组织政策核对当前的<a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001338-managing-chatgpt-sites-for-your-workspace\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">托管、数据驻留和数据要求（需联网）</a>。</p> <p><a id=\"visitor-privacy-and-personal-information\"></a></p> <h3 id=\"_17\">访客隐私和个人信息</h3> <p>添加表单、登录功能或其他收集访客信息的方式之前，请先确定站点为什么需要这些信息，以及会如何使用。审核谁可以访问提交内容、站点是否会将信息发送到其他服务，或向其他访客展示这些信息。</p> <p>规划合适的隐私声明和访客问题联系渠道。请与组织的隐私审核人员一起查看下列资源，确定声明和处理流程应覆盖哪些内容。</p> <p>有关责任和隐私指南，请查看：</p> <ul> <li><a href=\"https://openai.com/policies/chatgpt-sites-terms/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ChatGPT Sites 条款（需联网）</a>。</li> <li><a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001337-understanding-responsibilities-for-your-chatgpt-sites\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">运营者责任（需联网）</a>。</li> <li><a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001340\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">数据保护责任（需联网）</a>。</li> <li><a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001341\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">隐私政策指南（需联网）</a>。</li> </ul> <p><span id=\"what-happens-when-a-site-is-deleted-or-an-owner-leaves\"></span></p> <p><strong>如何规划所有权交接或站点退役？</strong></p> <p>在所有者更换岗位或离开之前，请确定由谁维护站点，并根据当前管理指南了解可用的交接方式。规划站点退役时，请安排如何限制访问以及如何处理站点收集的信息。承诺恢复或删除的时间表之前，先确认适用的删除和保留要求。</p> <p><span id=\"how-long-is-site-data-retained-including-backups\"></span></p> <p><a id=\"-1\"></a> <strong>在哪里可以确认数据保留和删除要求？</strong></p> <p>如有数据保留或删除方面的问题，请<a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/6614161-how-can-i-contact-support\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">联系 OpenAI 支持（需联网）</a>。</p> <p><span id=\"how-do-i-report-an-access-issue-or-incident\"></span></p> <p><a id=\"-2\"></a> <strong>如何报告访问问题？</strong></p> <p>如有访问方面的问题，请<a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/6614161-how-can-i-contact-support\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">联系 OpenAI 支持（需联网）</a>。请提供站点 URL、工作区、受影响的角色、尝试执行的操作、发生时间和错误详情。不要提供密钥或访问令牌。</p>","en":"<h1 id=\"sites-administration\">Sites administration</h1> <blockquote> <p>For the complete documentation index, see <a href=\"https://learn.chatgpt.com/llms.txt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">llms.txt (online)</a>. Markdown versions of documentation pages are available by appending <code>.md</code> to the page URL.</p> </blockquote> <p>For current workspace setup, permissions, and availability, see <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001338-managing-chatgpt-sites-for-your-workspace\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Managing ChatGPT Sites for your workspace (online)</a> in the Help Center.</p> <p><span id=\"what-costs-and-limits-should-we-plan-for\"></span></p> <p>Use this guide to decide who can create, change, and use sites in your workspace, and how your organization reviews and maintains them. To build or use a site, see <a href=\"#/article/a-3c6726ad641677d6\">Sites</a>.</p> <h2 id=\"before-you-begin\">Before you begin</h2> <p>Sign in to the workspace you administer. Decide who should create and publish sites, which audiences are appropriate, and who will handle review, support, and removal requests.</p> <p>Start with a specific use case. For example, an onboarding site that displays approved reference material needs a different review from a request tracker that collects employee information or a dashboard that uses connected apps. Agree on:</p> <ul> <li>The intended audience and whether external or public access is appropriate.</li> <li>The information the site displays, collects, stores, or sends to another service.</li> <li>The people responsible for the site's content, application behavior, and ongoing support.</li> <li>The review required before first publication and after material changes.</li> </ul> <h2 id=\"how-sites-work\">How sites work</h2> <p>A site can contain pages, application code, and visitor information. Review what it publishes and collects before sharing it. For the building and publishing workflow, see <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001339\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Creating and managing ChatGPT Sites (online)</a>.</p> <h3 id=\"what-you-can-build\">What you can build</h3> <p>Sites can support reference pages, forms, trackers, and interactive dashboards. The review should follow what the application does. A reference page needs a content and audience review. A form also needs a decision about which submissions to collect and who can read them. A connected dashboard needs a review of the accounts, permissions, and information used by its connections.</p> <h3 id=\"information-stored-or-exposed-by-a-site\">Information stored or exposed by a site</h3> <p>Information from prompts, conversation context, and uploaded or referenced files can become part of a site. Check the resulting pages, sample records, downloads, and generated content for information that should not reach the intended audience.</p> <p>Distinguish information copied into a site from information retrieved through a connection. For example, an uploaded spreadsheet can become a shared snapshot. A visitor using their own connected account may have different access from the person who built the site. Test the resulting behavior; the creator's preview alone does not establish what another visitor can see.</p> <p>For the builder workflow, see <a href=\"#/article/a-e0f092cc982945c2\">Data and connections</a>.</p> <p>For hosting and processing providers, see OpenAI's <a href=\"https://openai.com/policies/sub-processor-list/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">sub-processor list (online)</a>.</p> <h2 id=\"enable-sites-and-permissions\">Enable sites and permissions</h2> <p><span id=\"enable-sites-and-choose-sharing-permissions\"></span></p> <p>Review three levels of control: workspace permissions, the site's sharing and editing permissions, and permissions implemented by the application.</p> <figure> <p class=\"media-note\">Three levels of control: workspace permissions govern creation and publishing, site permissions govern viewing and editing, and application code governs access to records and actions. · <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://developers.openai.com/images/codex/sites/controls.webp\">联网查看原始媒体</a></p> <figcaption>Three levels of control: workspace permissions govern creation and publishing, site permissions govern viewing and editing, and application code governs access to records and actions. · <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/images/codex/sites/controls.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open original media</a></figcaption> </figure> <h3 id=\"workspace-admin-controls\">Workspace admin controls</h3> <p>Workspace permissions govern who can create and publish sites. Review public publishing and external invitations separately: allowing a named external viewer and making a site available to anyone on the internet are different decisions.</p> <p>For configuration steps and current role permissions, follow the <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001338-managing-chatgpt-sites-for-your-workspace\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">workspace administration guidance (online)</a>.</p> <h3 id=\"site-owner-and-collaborator-controls\">Site owner and collaborator controls</h3> <p>Use the site's sharing controls to decide who can view it and who can help maintain it.</p> <h4 id=\"audience-and-sharing\">Audience and sharing</h4> <p>A site's audience controls who can open it. Choose an audience that matches the information and activities the site provides. Review the full set of access grants when narrowing access. Removing an individual invitation may still leave access through a group, workspace-wide sharing, or public publishing.</p> <p>Check the site from the intended visitor experience after changing access. For the sharing workflow, see <a href=\"#/article/a-fc9cd7880ec6ce59\">Publishing and sharing</a>.</p> <h4 id=\"editors-and-collaboration\">Editors and collaboration</h4> <p>Owners can invite eligible workspace members as editors. Owners publish first; editors can publish later updates. Owners manage access and owner-only settings.</p> <p>Agree on how editors request and record review before publishing changes. An editor can publish later versions without a separate owner-approval step, so an organization's review process must account for who has edit access.</p> <h3 id=\"application-permissions\">Application permissions</h3> <p>Site access and access to individual records are separate. For private application data, enforce record access in server-side code and test with ordinary visitor accounts.</p> <p>For example, a request tracker might let every workspace member open the site while allowing each person to see only their own requests. The application needs to check that rule when returning a record or accepting an action. Hiding a page element does not enforce the rule. Test with separate accounts that should and should not have access to the same record.</p> <h2 id=\"configure-connections\">Configure connections</h2> <p><span id=\"enable-tenant-access-to-connected-apps\"></span></p> <p><span id=\"check-tenant-access-if-plugins-are-blocked\"></span></p> <p><span id=\"allow-an-additional-destination-for-one-site\"></span> <span id=\"credentials-and-connected-service-access\"></span></p> <p>Creating or sharing a site does not itself authorize connected-app access. Review organization approval, plugin settings, and each visitor's connected account and permissions in the <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001338-managing-chatgpt-sites-for-your-workspace\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Help Center connected-app guidance (online)</a>.</p> <p>Include connections in the site's review. Identify which services it uses, what information it requests, and who should authorize that access. Keep the site's audience decision separate from the connected service's permissions.</p> <p>Keep credentials out of prompts, attachments, and visible site content. For questions about network access, contact your workspace admin.</p> <h3 id=\"verify-the-visitor-experience\">Verify the visitor experience</h3> <p>Keep connected sites private to their workspace. Before sharing a connected site more widely within that workspace, test with another eligible workspace member who uses their own connected account. Confirm that the site shows information appropriate to that person's existing permissions.</p> <p>Also check the experience when a visitor does not grant the requested connection access. Explain which features need the connection and make sure the site does not present missing data as a successful result. Follow the current Help Center guidance for supported connections and access requirements.</p> <h2 id=\"review-and-manage-sites\">Review and manage sites</h2> <p><span id=\"workspace-visibility-and-audit-records\"></span> <span id=\"publishing-approval-and-network-controls\"></span></p> <h3 id=\"saved-versions-and-publishing\">Saved versions and publishing</h3> <p>Editing and saving a version are separate from publishing it. Review the updated preview, then publish when it's ready for visitors. Sending editing instructions does not immediately change the published site.</p> <figure> <p class=\"media-note\">Recommended workflow: edit and save, review and test, explicitly publish the version, then check the live site as a visitor. Review is an organizational step, not a product approval gate. · <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://developers.openai.com/images/codex/sites/publishing.webp\">联网查看原始媒体</a></p> <figcaption>Recommended workflow: edit and save, review and test, explicitly publish the version, then check the live site as a visitor. Review is an organizational step, not a product approval gate. · <a href=\"https://developers.openai.com/images/codex/sites/publishing.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Open original media</a></figcaption> </figure> <p>Use this workflow for the first publication and later updates:</p> <ol> <li>Describe the change and review the saved version.</li> <li>Check the content, audience, application permissions, and connections affected by the change.</li> <li>Complete your organization's review, then have the owner or an eligible editor publish the version. The owner must complete the first publication.</li> <li>Open the published site as an intended visitor and confirm that the expected content and behavior are available.</li> </ol> <p>The review step is a recommended organizational process. It does not add a product approval gate.</p> <h3 id=\"shared-responsibilities\">Shared responsibilities</h3> <p>Successful generation or publication does not establish that a site meets your organization's requirements. Assign responsibility for reviewing the content, access rules, generated application code, and connected services. Use reviewers with the appropriate expertise for the site's data and use case.</p> <h3 id=\"review-before-publishing\">Review before publishing</h3> <p>Review the intended audience, generated code, visitor submissions, and connections before publishing. Test the experience for intended and unintended visitors. Repeat the review after material changes.</p> <p>For example, a team dashboard can look correct for its creator while exposing an uploaded customer list to a broader audience than intended. A request form can collect the right fields while allowing one visitor to read another visitor's submission. Review both what appears on the page and how the application handles data and actions.</p> <p>Before publishing, check:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Content:</strong> Review generated text, images, links, uploaded files, and example data for accuracy and appropriate sharing.</li> <li><strong>Audience:</strong> Confirm who should be able to open the site, including external viewers and public visitors where applicable.</li> <li><strong>Records and actions:</strong> Verify that visitors can read and change only the information they have permission to access.</li> <li><strong>Forms and submissions:</strong> Decide what to collect, where it goes, who can see it, and how visitors can ask questions about it.</li> <li><strong>Connections and credentials:</strong> Review the services used, requested access, and any information exposed in pages, downloads, or error messages.</li> <li><strong>Visitor experience:</strong> Use separate visitor accounts to check the expected paths and denied-access cases.</li> </ul> <h3 id=\"ongoing-maintenance\">Ongoing maintenance</h3> <p>Give each production site an owner and a reporting route for access, data, or service issues. Plan ownership handoffs and retirement with your workspace admin.</p> <p>Revisit the review when the site gains a new audience, editor, data source, connection, form, or application feature. Periodically check whether the audience and editor list still match the site's purpose, whether its content is current, and whether its connections still work for intended visitors.</p> <h3 id=\"troubleshoot-access\">Troubleshoot access</h3> <p>Start with the affected person's account, role, and attempted action. A creator's successful preview does not confirm that another person can view, edit, publish, or use a connection.</p> <table> <thead> <tr> <th>Symptom</th> <th>What to check</th> </tr> </thead> <tbody> <tr> <td>A member cannot create or publish a site</td> <td>Confirm the active workspace, feature availability, and permissions for that member's role.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>A visitor cannot open a site</td> <td>Check the site's audience and whether the visitor is signed in with the account that received access.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>An editor cannot publish</td> <td>Check edit access, workspace permissions, and whether the owner has completed the first publication.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>A connection works for the creator but not another visitor</td> <td>Confirm that the site is private to its workspace and the visitor belongs to that workspace. Check the visitor's connected account, service permissions, authorization, and the applicable organization and plugin settings.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>A person can still view a site after an invitation is removed</td> <td>Review remaining group, workspace-wide, or public access.</td> </tr> <tr> <td>A published site does not show an expected edit</td> <td>Confirm that the intended version was published, then open the live site to check the result.</td> </tr> </tbody> </table> <p>To review available workspace controls or troubleshoot access, follow the <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001338-managing-chatgpt-sites-for-your-workspace\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Help Center guidance (online)</a>.</p> <h2 id=\"security-and-privacy\">Security and privacy</h2> <p><span id=\"certifications-encryption-and-customer-managed-keys\"></span></p> <p><span id=\"applicable-terms-and-data-processing-agreements\"></span></p> <p>Check current <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001338-managing-chatgpt-sites-for-your-workspace\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">hosting, residency, and data requirements (online)</a> against your organization's policies before choosing what information to store in a site.</p> <h3 id=\"visitor-privacy-and-personal-information\">Visitor privacy and personal information</h3> <p>Before adding a form, sign-in feature, or other way to collect visitor information, decide why the site needs that information and how the site will use it. Review who can access submissions and whether the site will send information to another service or display it to other visitors.</p> <p>Plan the appropriate privacy notice and a contact route for visitor questions. Use the resources below with your organization's privacy reviewers to determine what the notice and handling process should cover.</p> <p>For responsibilities and privacy guidance, review these resources:</p> <ul> <li>Review <a href=\"https://openai.com/policies/chatgpt-sites-terms/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">ChatGPT Sites Terms (online)</a>.</li> <li>Review <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001337-understanding-responsibilities-for-your-chatgpt-sites\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Operator responsibilities (online)</a>.</li> <li>Review <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001340\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Data-protection responsibilities (online)</a>.</li> <li>Review <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001341\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Privacy-policy guidance (online)</a>.</li> </ul> <p><span id=\"what-happens-when-a-site-is-deleted-or-an-owner-leaves\"></span></p> <p><a id=\"how-should-i-plan-an-ownership-handoff-or-site-retirement\"></a> <strong>How should I plan an ownership handoff or site retirement?</strong></p> <p>Before an owner changes roles or leaves, identify who will maintain the site and use the current administration guidance for available handoff options. For retirement, plan how to restrict access and handle information collected by the site. Confirm deletion and retention requirements before promising a recovery or removal timeline.</p> <p><span id=\"how-long-is-site-data-retained-including-backups\"></span></p> <p><a id=\"where-can-i-confirm-retention-and-deletion-requirements\"></a> <strong>Where can I confirm retention and deletion requirements?</strong></p> <p>For retention or deletion questions, <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/6614161-how-can-i-contact-support\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">contact OpenAI Support (online)</a>.</p> <p><span id=\"how-do-i-report-an-access-issue-or-incident\"></span></p> <p><a id=\"how-do-i-report-an-access-issue\"></a> <strong>How do I report an access issue?</strong></p> <p>For access questions, <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/6614161-how-can-i-contact-support\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">contact OpenAI Support (online)</a>. Include the site URL, workspace, affected role, attempted action, time, and error details. Omit secrets and access tokens.</p>"};
